-- title:
--   Tensor product of invertible ideal sheaves is the product ideal
-- statement:
--   Let $X$ be a scheme and let $I$, $J$ be ideal sheaf data on $X$, each assumed invertible in the sense of the project predicate `IsInvertible`: for every point $x$ of $X$ there are an affine open $U$ of $X$, a section $f \in \Gamma(X, U)$ with $x \in X.\mathrm{basicOpen}\, f$, and an element $g$ of the monoid of non-zero-divisors of $\Gamma(X, X.\mathrm{affineBasicOpen}\, f)$ such that the ideal cut out by $I$ (resp. $J$) on $X.\mathrm{affineBasicOpen}\, f$ is the principal ideal generated by $g$. Here, for an ideal sheaf datum $I$, the $\mathcal{O}_X$-module $I.\mathrm{module}$ is the kernel of the canonical map from the unit sheaf of modules $\mathbf{1} = \mathcal{O}_X$ to the pushforward along the closed immersion $I.\mathrm{subschemeι}$ of the unit sheaf of the closed subscheme, and $I.\mathrm{moduleι}$ is its inclusion into $\mathbf{1}$ (on an affine open its sections map is injective with image the ideal of $I$). The assertion is that there exists an isomorphism $\mu \colon I.\mathrm{module} \otimes J.\mathrm{module} \cong (I \cdot J).\mathrm{module}$ of $\mathcal{O}_X$-modules such that $\mu$ followed by the inclusion $(I \cdot J).\mathrm{moduleι}$ equals $I.\mathrm{moduleι} \otimes J.\mathrm{moduleι}$ followed by the left unitor $\mathbf{1} \otimes \mathbf{1} \xrightarrow{\sim} \mathbf{1}$; that is, $\mu$ is compatible with multiplication of sections inside $\mathcal{O}_X$.
--
--   This is the canonical form of the classical identification $\mathcal{O}(-D) \otimes \mathcal{O}(-E) \cong \mathcal{O}(-D-E)$ for effective Cartier divisors, the isomorphism being pinned down by the requirement that it be induced by multiplication of sections in $\mathcal{O}_X$. In this form it is used to build the $I$-adic filtration and thickening exact sequences attached to an invertible ideal sheaf, and thence in the computations of cohomology and ranks for relative Picard functors of curves.
